<!-- INTRODUCTORY COPY -->
<div class="acdt-intro">
  <p>
    Welcome to our dedicated guide and converter for <strong>GMT vs CDT</strong>. Whether you are coordinating an international conference between London and Chicago, scheduling software deployments across North American data centers, or booking international flights, understanding the exact relationship between Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) and Central Daylight Time (CDT) is essential.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong> serves as the astronomical standard based on the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London at <strong>UTC+0</strong>. GMT does not shift for daylight saving time. <strong>Central Daylight Time (CDT)</strong> is the seasonal daylight-saving offset at <strong>UTC−5</strong> observed across the North American Central corridor—including major metropolitan areas like Chicago, Dallas-Fort Worth, Houston, Minneapolis, and Winnipeg. Because CDT runs 5 hours behind GMT, calculating the time difference is straightforward: subtract 5 hours from GMT to obtain CDT, or add 5 hours to CDT to obtain GMT.
  </p>
  <div class="seo-keyword-intro">
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> During the daylight saving months (mid-March to early November), the difference between <strong>GMT and CDT</strong> is exactly <strong>5 hours</strong>. Outside this window, when the Central zone returns to <a href="/gmt-to-cst/">Central Standard Time (CST, UTC−6)</a>, the difference becomes <strong>6 hours</strong>. Use our real-time converter above and the detailed 24-hour conversion grid below to convert hours with calendar date rollover accuracy.
  </div>
</div>

