<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     CDT VS CST CLARIFICATION
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="cdt-vs-cst" class="reveal">
  <h2>CDT vs CST: Key Differences for Central Time</h2>
  <p>
    A frequent point of confusion when searching <strong>GMT vs CDT</strong> is conflating Central Daylight Time (CDT) with Central Standard Time (CST). While both apply to the North American Central corridor, they operate under different UTC offsets and calendar seasons:
  </p>

  <div class="method-grid" style="margin-top: 18px;">
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Central Daylight Time (CDT)</h3>
      <code>UTC−5 Offset</code>
      <p>
        <strong>Active Season:</strong> Second Sunday in March to the first Sunday in November (summer daylight saving time).
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 8px;">
        <strong>GMT Difference:</strong> GMT is <strong>5 hours ahead</strong> of CDT (<code>CDT = GMT − 5</code>).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Central Standard Time (CST)</h3>
      <code>UTC−6 Offset</code>
      <p>
        <strong>Active Season:</strong> First Sunday in November to the second Sunday in March (winter standard time).
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 8px;">
        <strong>GMT Difference:</strong> GMT is <strong>6 hours ahead</strong> of CST (<code>CST = GMT − 6</code>).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Avoiding 1-Hour Meeting Errors</h3>
      <code>Crucial Scheduling Note</code>
      <p>
        Specifying "CST" for an event scheduled in July refers to a time 1 hour behind actual clocks in Chicago and Dallas. Always verify whether CDT or CST is in effect.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 8px;">
        Need winter conversions? Visit our <a href="/gmt-to-cst/" style="color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline;">GMT to CST Converter</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

