<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="source-notes" class="source-notes reveal">
  <h2>Common Confusions: GMT, CDT, CST, and UTC Explained</h2>
  <p>
    Timezone abbreviations are easily mixed up. Here is a clear breakdown of the terminology:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>CDT vs CST</strong>: Central Daylight Time (<a href="/cdt/">CDT</a>) is UTC−5 and observed from March to November. Central Standard Time (<a href="/cst/">CST</a>) is UTC−6 and observed from November to March. Scheduling an event using CST in summer results in a 1-hour error relative to actual clocks in Chicago.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GMT vs UTC</strong>: Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is the historic astronomical time baseline, while Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) is the atomic time standard. For civil scheduling and everyday conversions, their offsets are identical (+0).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Central Time (CT)</strong>: "Central Time" is an umbrella term encompassing both CDT and CST. It automatically refers to whichever offset is active on the date in question.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CDT vs EST</strong>: Central Daylight Time (CDT) and Eastern Standard Time (EST) both operate at UTC−5. However, they occur in different seasons. When CDT is active in summer, the Eastern zone observes Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC−4), remaining 1 hour ahead. Learn more in our <a href="/time-zone-guide/">Time Zone Guide</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

